join after dedup** | After `ROW_NUMBER` picks `rn=1`, the outer query joins `repo_commits + repos + repo_checkpoints` *again*, even though those columns were already in scope inside the inner CTE. Wasted IO that scales with the post-dedup row count. |\n| 4 | **Sequential awaits in `mergeAnalysesInto`** | `await mergeAnalysesInto(db, meByAgent, my)` then `await mergeAnalysesInto(db, teamByAgent, team)`. Two sets of analysis queries that could run in parallel — easy `Promise.all`. |\n| 5 | **PlanetScale round-trip latency** | Local wrangler → `aws.connect.psdb.cloud` adds ~30–80ms per query. With 4 sequential phases (me-cp, team-cp parallel; me-analyses, team-analyses sequential), you're paying ~200ms in pure RTT. Smallest contributor — but free to halve with parallelization. |\n| 6 | **The CLI's `limit=500`** | The CLI passes `limit=500` for almost every range. Useful for me-side (caps cross-repo recap), pointless for the per-repo recap call where you'd already have at most ~50 checkpoints. The handler clamps to 1000 max. |\n\n## Quick wins (server side, ordered by ROI)\n\n1. **Run `EXPLAIN`** on both queries against staging. If `repo_commits` is missing `(commit_author_github_id, commit_date)` or `(repo_id, commit_author_github_id, commit_date)`, add it. This alone could 5-10× the queries.\n2. **`Promise.all` the two `mergeAnalysesInto` calls** — three-line change, saves one full round-trip set.\n3. **Push `LIMIT` and `ORDER BY` into the inner CTE** — apply it before the outer join, so the post-dedup work touches at most 500 rows.\n4. **Replace the window function with a `GROUP BY (repo_id, checkpoint_id)`** picking max(default_branch_match) — sometimes the MySQL planner handles GROUP BY better than ROW_NUMBER, especially with proper indexes. Worth A/B'ing.\n5. **Cache the response per (user, repo, range)** with a short TTL (5 min) — the dashboard already accepts staleness this big.\n6. **For ranges > 30 days, pre-compute via background rollup**. The recap is descri