ted on push, harmless.\"\n\n## Suggestions\n\n- Task 2.1: Add the `AnalysisFacetCount` type export to a shared types file the route can import too, so Chunk 5's helper doesn't end up with an inline duplicate type.\n- Task 2.5: Add an `EXPLAIN` step right after the impl lands locally to confirm the composite-IN actually uses `PRIMARY` (not full-table scan). Cheap; catches issue #1 immediately.\n- Task 3.2: Add a test for the empty-input case (analysis with `extraction.labels = []`) — backfill semantics rely on this.\n- Task 5.2: Worth a single sanity test that the route still works when `aggregateFacetsByAgent` returns an empty Map (zero-checkpoint range). The current plan only tests the populated case.\n- Add a top-level \"prerequisites\" section listing exact commands an agent should run to confirm: which Kysely version, which test-db helper exists, which backfill template to mirror. This kills several of the \"...\" holes at once.\n- Task 7.5: Consider running the perf check from a non-laptop network too (your local network might mask CF cold-start variance).\n\n## Strengths\n\n- Clear success thresholds per range with explicit decision gates.\n- Good TDD cadence in Chunk 2 (red → green → commit per method).\n- Idempotency carefully called out for upsertFacets and backfill.\n- Atomic transaction wrapping for upsertFacets prevents partial-state reads.\n- Multi-PR scope and rollout sequencing (schema → backfill → code → CLI ready) is correct.\n- Sharded chunks roughly map to commits; commit messages are pre-written and good.\n- Composite PK design `(repo_id, checkpoint_id, name)` plus `(repo_id, name)` covering index is the right shape for the workload.\n\nNet: solid plan structurally but with 4-5 concrete blockers (#1, #3, #5, #7, #8, #13) that will stop a fresh agent cold. Address those and this is approvable."}],"totalDurationMs":71063,"totalTokens":56866,"totalToolUseCount":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":29252,"cache_read_input_tokens"