{"parentUuid":"d6f075e8-cd77-43c1-9623-e480c23c5cb0","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"ff55b726-7a1f-4a98-9f9a-de011008b694","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fx1CWo1LjsZ55ArRkMsV2Y","type":"tool_result","content":"# Cloudflare WARP Setup\n\nWe use Cloudflare WARP to access internal applications (e.g. librarian, admin panels). WARP connects your device to Entire's Cloudflare Zero Trust network, so you don't need a traditional VPN.\n\nWhen WARP is on, all your internet traffic routes through Cloudflare's network. This means:\n- You get access to internal apps without extra login steps\n- CLI tools (e.g. `curl`, custom CLIs) work seamlessly with protected endpoints -- no extra headers or tokens needed\n\nWe don't currently have any Gateway filtering policies (DNS, HTTP, or network). Your traffic passes through Cloudflare but nothing is blocked or inspected. WARP is used purely for identity-based access to internal apps right now. Note that Gateway traffic metadata (DNS queries, connection logs) may be logged by Cloudflare even without active filtering policies.\n\n## Install\n\nDownload and install the Cloudflare WARP client for your platform:\n\n- **macOS**: https://developers.cloudflare.com/cloudflare-one/team-and-resources/devices/cloudflare-one-client/deployment/manual-deployment/\n- **Windows / Linux**: same page, pick your platform\n\n## Enrol your device\n\n1. Open the WARP client\n2. Go to **Preferences** (or **Settings**)\n3. **Account** > **Login to Cloudflare Zero Trust**\n4. Enter the team name: `entireio`\n5. A browser window will open -- sign in with your `@entire.io` Google Workspace account\n6. Once authenticated, WARP will connect and show the Entire org name\n\nThat's it. Internal apps should now be accessible in your browser and from the CLI.\n\n## Toggling WARP\n\nYou can turn WARP on and off via the client. When WARP is off, you lose access to internal apps behind Cloudflare Access.\n\nIf you're on a network with a captive portal (hotel, airport), WARP will temporarily disable itself so you can log in to the network.\n\n## Kubernetes access\n\nWith WARP on, `kubectl` works against the staging EKS cluster through a Cloudflare Tunnel -- no VPN or IP allowlists needed. Just configure kubectl as usual:\n\n```sh\naws eks update-kubeconfig --name eks-staging --region us-east-2\nkubectl get nodes\n```\n\nTraffic flows: your machine → WARP → Cloudflare → tunnel → private EKS endpoint. The EKS API hostname is resolved via split DNS to the private VPC IPs.\n\n## Network notes\n\nWARP routes traffic for the following `10.x` ranges through a Cloudflare Tunnel into the staging VPC:\n\n- `10.0.0.0/16` -- current staging VPC\n- `10.32.0.0/11` and above -- reserved for future VPCs\n\nThe `10.1.0.0 - 10.31.255.255` range is excluded so home LAN traffic (common on `10.x` routers) isn't affected.\n\nIf your home network uses `10.0.0.x`, you may experience connectivity issues with WARP on. Switching your router to `192.168.x.x` will fix this. See FND-32 for the plan to move the VPC to a high `10.x` range.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n- **Can't enrol**: make sure you're using your `@entire.io` Google account, not a personal one\n- **Internal app not loading**: check WARP is connected (green icon in the menu bar)\n- **Captive portal not working**: WARP should auto-detect and pause for 3 minutes. If not, toggle WARP off, connect to WiFi, then toggle back on\n- **kubectl timeout**: make sure WARP is on. If still failing, toggle WARP off/on to refresh the tunnel route","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"547aa7d0-1d6c-4c42-af63-2057396c1d39","timestamp":"2026-04-29T12:59:06.424Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# Cloudflare WARP Setup\n\nWe use Cloudflare WARP to access internal applications (e.g. librarian, admin panels). WARP connects your device to Entire's Cloudflare Zero Trust network, so you don't need a traditional VPN.\n\nWhen WARP is on, all your internet traffic routes through Cloudflare's network. This means:\n- You get access to internal apps without extra login steps\n- CLI tools (e.g. `curl`, custom CLIs) work seamlessly with protected endpoints -- no extra headers or tokens needed\n\nWe don't currently have any Gateway filtering policies (DNS, HTTP, or network). Your traffic passes through Cloudflare but nothing is blocked or inspected. WARP is used purely for identity-based access to internal apps right now. Note that Gateway traffic metadata (DNS queries, connection logs) may be logged by Cloudflare even without active filtering policies.\n\n## Install\n\nDownload and install the Cloudflare WARP client for your platform:\n\n- **macOS**: https://developers.cloudflare.com/cloudflare-one/team-and-resources/devices/cloudflare-one-client/deployment/manual-deployment/\n- **Windows / Linux**: same page, pick your platform\n\n## Enrol your device\n\n1. Open the WARP client\n2. Go to **Preferences** (or **Settings**)\n3. **Account** > **Login to Cloudflare Zero Trust**\n4. Enter the team name: `entireio`\n5. A browser window will open -- sign in with your `@entire.io` Google Workspace account\n6. Once authenticated, WARP will connect and show the Entire org name\n\nThat's it. Internal apps should now be accessible in your browser and from the CLI.\n\n## Toggling WARP\n\nYou can turn WARP on and off via the client. When WARP is off, you lose access to internal apps behind Cloudflare Access.\n\nIf you're on a network with a captive portal (hotel, airport), WARP will temporarily disable itself so you can log in to the network.\n\n## Kubernetes access\n\nWith WARP on, `kubectl` works against the staging EKS cluster through a Cloudflare Tunnel -- no VPN or IP allowlists needed. Just configure kubectl as usual:\n\n```sh\naws eks update-kubeconfig --name eks-staging --region us-east-2\nkubectl get nodes\n```\n\nTraffic flows: your machine → WARP → Cloudflare → tunnel → private EKS endpoint. The EKS API hostname is resolved via split DNS to the private VPC IPs.\n\n## Network notes\n\nWARP routes traffic for the following `10.x` ranges through a Cloudflare Tunnel into the staging VPC:\n\n- `10.0.0.0/16` -- current staging VPC\n- `10.32.0.0/11` and above -- reserved for future VPCs\n\nThe `10.1.0.0 - 10.31.255.255` range is excluded so home LAN traffic (common on `10.x` routers) isn't affected.\n\nIf your home network uses `10.0.0.x`, you may experience connectivity issues with WARP on. Switching your router to `192.168.x.x` will fix this. See FND-32 for the plan to move the VPC to a high `10.x` range.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n- **Can't enrol**: make sure you're using your `@entire.io` Google account, not a personal one\n- **Internal app not loading**: check WARP is connected (green icon in the menu bar)\n- **Captive portal not working**: WARP should auto-detect and pause for 3 minutes. If not, toggle WARP off, connect to WiFi, then toggle back on\n- **kubectl timeout**: make sure WARP is on. If still failing, toggle WARP off/on to refresh the tunnel route","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d6f075e8-cd77-43c1-9623-e480c23c5cb0","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/entire-activity","sessionId":"a654b6ca-4957-4d7c-8c99-62db313ff0a0","version":"2.1.119","gitBranch":"entire-activity"}
