/checkpoints.ts` (`getCheckpointsByUser` `repoFullName` filter at line ~1701, and `getTeamCheckpointsOnUserRepos` introduced at line 1791) ship with **zero direct unit tests** — coverage rests entirely on the route-level mocks, which means the SQL itself (joins, dedup, the `userRepoIds` subquery, the `commit_author_github_id != githubId` filter, the new `repos.full_name` predicate) is unverified.\n\n---\n\n### Critical Gaps (8-10)\n\n**1. New SQL functions have no tests at all — `api/src/lib/planetscale/checkpoints.ts:1670` (`getCheckpointsByUser` repoFullName branch) and `api/src/lib/planetscale/checkpoints.ts:1791` (`getTeamCheckpointsOnUserRepos`).** Criticality 9.\n- The whole point of the PR comment at `checkpoints.ts:1671-1675` (\"fixes the bug where cross-repo activity could push the requested repo's checkpoints out of the result set\") is a SQL-LIMIT-ordering invariant. There is no test that proves `repoFullName` is applied before `LIMIT` or that the repo-scoped LIMIT actually lets a smaller-volume requested repo survive.\n- `getTeamCheckpointsOnUserRepos` has a non-trivial `userRepoIds` subquery (`checkpoints.ts:1804-1810`) plus a `commit_author_github_id != githubId` filter (`checkpoints.ts:1836`) and a `commit_author_github_id is not null` filter (`checkpoints.ts:1837`). All three are silent-failure-prone if the SQL is later refactored — e.g., dropping `is not null` would silently include bot-author checkpoints, dropping the inequality would double-count the user's own checkpoints on the team side. None of these are caught by the route tests because they mock `getTeamOnUserRepos` directly.\n- The `user_repos` join (`checkpoints.ts:1820-1822`) silently scopes the team set to repos the user has access to. If that join is removed, the endpoint leaks team activity from repos the user shouldn't see — a privacy regression. The route-level mocks bypass this entirely.\n\n**2. Error/exception path returns 500 — uncovered. `recap.ts:288-292`.** Criticality 8.\n- No