ByCheckpointSet` issues N parallel SQL queries per request via `aggregateFacetsByAgent`** — `api/src/lib/planetscale/analyses.ts:284-303`\n\nFor ~5 agents × 4 facet kinds + 1 scalar = 25 queries fired in parallel per `/me/recap` call. With the `(repo_id, checkpoint_id) IN ((..),(..),...)` clause taking the full per-agent checkpoint set, each query already filters server-side. This is fine for the stated 1k-checkpoint workload but the approach scales poorly above ~50 agents or 10k checkpoints because each query repeats the tuple list. Not a blocker — the current sizes are far below that — but flag for a follow-up: aggregator could do a single query joining facet tables to `repo_checkpoints` with `agent` in the GROUP BY. Confidence 80.\n\n**3. `mergeFacetsIntoAggregates` silently drops unknown agents** — `api/src/routes/recap.ts:506-508`\n\n```ts\nconst agg = byAgent[agent]\nif (!agg) continue\n```\n\nIf the SQL aggregator returns an agent name not present in `byAgent` (e.g. capitalization drift between `repo_checkpoints.agent` and the bucket initialization), its facets are dropped silently. The legacy `mergeAnalysesInto` had the same behavior, so it's not a regression — but with the SQL path the agent-name mismatch surface is wider (the aggregator now joins against `repo_checkpoints` server-side). Add a `console.warn` or telemetry counter for the drop case. Confidence 82.\n\n## Minor (not reported per ≥80 rule, included briefly)\n\n- `USE_FACET_AGGREGATOR = true` const in `api/src/routes/recap.ts:41` — the comment says \"REMOVE AFTER 24h PRODUCTION SOAK\" but the flag is a hardcoded `const`, not an env var, so rollback requires a redeploy. The PR description says the same. Acceptable as documented but the comment \"Toggle this off to revert ... without redeploying\" contradicts the implementation.\n- `USE_FACET_AGGREGATOR` should ship behind a real env/KV flag if rollback agility matters. Confidence 70.\n\n## Strengths\n\n- **Atomicity**: `upsertFacets` wraps DELETE+