{"parentUuid":"0e079834-c5eb-445b-b5c9-927d3eb7b98e","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"e7a75260-cc82-420c-a2c9-2365e9989cc4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YYPCaDtPVzLuxLU7ftbwT3","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No casts, no named row types — the facet table row types are referenced only via the generated kysely `DB` schema.\n\n## Type Design Analysis: PR #1737 (`feat/recap-facets-sql-agg`)\n\n### Type: Facet Row Types (`RepoCheckpointAnalysisLabels`, `…Skills`, `…ToolMix`, `…Mcp`)\n\nThese come from the kysely-generated `api/db/schema.ts` — there are no hand-authored `FacetLabelRow` / `FacetSkillRow` / `FacetToolMixRow` / `FacetMcpRow` types in the PR. The handler/namespace boundary normalizes everything to `AnalysisFacetCount = { name; count }` and `AnalysisFacetsByAgent` (camelCase). DB-shape types never escape `analyses.ts`.\n\n**Encapsulation: 4/5.** Snake_case columns (`repo_id`, `checkpoint_id`, `transcript_tokens`, `files_changed`) stay inside the namespace; the public API is camelCase (`AnalysisFacetsByAgent`, `transcriptTokens`, `filesChanged`). The one leak is `AnalysisFacetCheckpointRef` — `{ repo_id; checkpoint_id }` is exported on `AnalysesNamespace` and the caller in `recap.ts` constructs it using DB field names. Minor; route-side checkpoint rows already use snake_case.\n\n**Invariant Expression: 2/5.** Four near-identical TS interfaces are generated and four parallel branches exist everywhere (migration, `upsertFacets`, `aggregateFacetsByCheckpointSet`'s ternary, schema `DB`). The shared `(repo_id, checkpoint_id, name, count, created_at)` shape is captured nowhere as a base type. The `name` field is `string` in every row type and in `AnalysisFacetCount`. The toolMix-name domain (`\"shell\" | \"fileOps\" | \"search\" | \"mcp\" | \"agent\" | \"other\"`) is a hardcoded `Set` at runtime in `mergeFacetsIntoAggregates` (recap.ts:506) — the type system sees only `string`. `AgentAggregate.toolMix` is correctly typed as a struct with those six keys, but the join between facet rows (string `name`) and that struct relies on a runtime `Set.has` check. A `type ToolMixCategory = \"shell\" | \"fileOps\" | …` literal-union, with `toolMix: Array<{ name: ToolMixCategory; count: number }>` on the SQL boundary, would push the invariant to compile time and let the cast `(agg.toolMix as Record<string, number>)[t.name] = t.count` go away.\n\n**Invariant Usefulness: 4/5.** `AnalysisFacetsByAgent` is genuinely shared: `aggregateFacetsByAgent` returns it and `mergeFacetsIntoAggregates` consumes it; the legacy `mergeAnalysesInto` writes into the same `AgentAggregate` shape so the two code paths cannot silently diverge (good — they hit the same struct fields). `AnalysisFacetKind = \"labels\" | \"skills\" | \"tool_mix\" | \"mcp\"` is a real string-literal union and gates the table ternary. The route-level `AgentAggregate` has its own field names (`labels: { label, count }`, `skills: { skill, count }`) which differ from `AnalysisFacetCount` (`{ name, count }`), forcing `.map((l) => ({ label: l.name, count: l.count }))` adapters in two places — a small but real source of drift if someone adds a field.\n\n**Invariant Enforcement: 4/5.** No `as any`. The tuple-IN parametrization uses `sql<boolean>` and `sql<number>` annotations and `sql.ref(\"count\")` to safely quote a reserved word — solid. The DELETE-then-INSERT in `upsertFacets` is wrapped in `db.transaction()`, with `if (length > 0)` guards (Kysely rejects empty `.values([])`). One narrow gap: in `aggregateFacetsByCheckpointSet` the `kind` ternary is exhaustive only because TS narrows the union — adding a 5th facet kind is not a type error (the final `else` falls through to `mcp`). A `satisfies Record<AnalysisFacetKind, TableName>` lookup map would make exhaustiveness compile-checked. `aggregateScalarsByCheckpointSet` is module-private (good encapsulation choice, called out in a comment). `Number(row.total)` casts defend agai