{"parentUuid":"1d71b2a0-fedd-493f-a4d8-c682a7ae9b2b","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"e7a75260-cc82-420c-a2c9-2365e9989cc4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GqVBcQfhFZR2r5hwntwfED","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Confirmed: toolMix categories are restricted to the same 6 keys at the producer side. Now I have enough to deliver the audit.\n\n# Error Handling Audit — PR #1737\n\nFindings on the perf refactor (facet tables + worker write path + `USE_FACET_AGGREGATOR` route gate + backfill). All paths are absolute to `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/entire.io/.worktrees/recap-endpoint/`.\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL — Worker write path: torn-write window between facets and scalars\n\n`api/src/lib/analysis-queue.ts:727-745` issues `db.analyses.upsertFacets(...)` (one transaction across 4 tables) and then `db.analyses.updateScalarAggregates(...)` as a **second, independent statement**. If the first succeeds and the second fails (network blip, request abort, marshalling error), the row is left with fresh facet rows but stale `transcript_tokens=0/files_changed=0`. Recap reads SUM across that mix and silently undercounts. The accompanying comment claims \"Idempotent — same UPDATE under retries yields the same final state,\" but the surrounding worker has no retry of just the scalar leg — `generateAndStoreReview` either succeeds end-to-end or restarts from the top (which re-runs `markComplete`, but `markComplete` returns `false` after the first success, gating both writes off — see test at line 655 \"does not call upsertFacets when markComplete returns false (superseded)\"). So a partial failure is **not self-healing** under retries.\n\nRecommendation: wrap both writes in a single Kysely transaction, or move scalars into the `upsertFacets` transaction. At minimum, log the `markComplete-succeeded-but-denormalize-failed` case with an error ID — there is currently **no try/catch around either call**, so the error bubbles, but `markComplete` already committed, leaving the inconsistent state.\n\n## CRITICAL — `mergeFacetsIntoAggregates` silently drops unknown toolMix names\n\n`api/src/routes/recap.ts:1757,1766-1770`:\n\n```ts\nconst toolMixKeys = new Set([\"shell\", \"fileOps\", \"search\", \"mcp\", \"agent\", \"other\"])\n...\nfor (const t of f.toolMix) {\n  if (toolMixKeys.has(t.name)) { (agg.toolMix as Record<string, number>)[t.name] = t.count }\n}\n```\n\nIf a future analysis pipeline emits a new category (`web`, `notebook`, etc.) the SQL tier writes it, the aggregator returns it, and the route **silently discards it** with no log, no metric, no error ID. The producer at `analysis-queue.ts:244` currently emits exactly those 6 keys, so today this is benign — but the contract is now split across two files with no compile-time tie. Add `logError(...)` (or at least `console.warn`) when a facet name falls outside `toolMixKeys`, and ideally derive the Set from a shared constant exported alongside the producer's `keys` tuple at `analysis-queue.ts:244`.\n\n## IMPORTANT — Backfill skip-and-continue swallows every error class\n\n`api/scripts/backfill-analysis-facets.ts:268-271`:\n\n```ts\n} catch (err) {\n  skipped++\n  console.warn(`skip repo=${row.repo_id} cp=${row.checkpoint_id}: ${err}`)\n}\n```\n\nThis `catch` covers **everything**: JSON parse failures (expected), DB connection drops (NOT expected — should abort), transient PlanetScale 5xx (should retry), schema mismatches (should abort and alert). A backfill that \"completed successfully\" with `skipped=1247` because PlanetScale flapped halfway through is the worst possible outcome — operators believe the table is hot when it has holes. Narrow the catch to `SyntaxError`/typed parse errors; let infra errors bubble. At minimum: exit non-zero if `skipped/processed > 0.01`.\n\n## IMPORTANT — `USE_FACET_AGGREGATOR` constant, not env-gated\n\n`api/src/routes/recap.ts:1693`: `const USE_FACET_AGGREGATOR = true` is a module-level boolean, not a runtime flag. The comment promises \"Toggle this off to revert ... without redeploying\" — that's false; flipping it requires a code edit + Worker redeploy. Either rename to `LEGACY_AGGREGATOR_ENABLED` and accept the deploy cost honestly, or wire it to `c.env` so ops can flip it. As written, the \"fast-rollback escape hatch\" is a comment promise the code doesn't keep.\n\n## IMPORTANT — Migration's index-creation catch: substring matching on error messages\n\n`api/db/migrations/20260429100000_add_analysis_facet_tables.ts:25-31` swallows errors whose message contains `\"Duplicate key name\"` or `\"already exists\"`. A driver upgrade that re-words the error (`\"index name conflicts\"`) silently re-throws; worse, an unrelated error containing the substring `already exists` (e.g., `\"table already exists\"` during a partial rollback) is silently absorbed. Prefer matching on MySQL error codes (`ER_DUP_KEYNAME` = 1061) via `err.code`/`err.errno`, not message text.\n\n## MINOR — Reserved-word handling: only `count` quoted\n\n`api/src/lib/planetscale/analyses.ts:1363` uses `sql.ref(\"count\")`. Schema for the four facet tables uses `repo_id`, `checkpoint_id`, `name`, `count`, `created_at` — `name` is a MySQL reserved word in some contexts (function name, not column), but `count`, `name`, and `created_at` are all bare identifiers in queries elsewhere (`groupBy(\"name\")`, `orderBy(\"total\")`). Today this works because Kysely auto-quotes column refs in `select`/`groupBy`, and only the raw `sql\\`SUM(${sql.ref(\"count\")})\\`` template needed manual quoting. No bug, but worth a one-line comment explaining why `count` is the only one wrapped, or just use \\`SUM(\\\\\\`count\\\\\\`)\\` literally.\n\n## MINOR — `updateScalarAggregates` no-op when row missing is undocumented to callers\n\n`api/src/lib/planetscale/analyses.ts:1335-1343` issues a plain `UPDATE` with no row check. Test at line 991 documents \"is a no-op when no analyses row exists\" — fine for the worker path (gated by `markComplete` success) but **the backfill** at `backfill-analysis-facets.ts:260` calls it on every row read from `repo_checkpoint_analyses`. If the row is concurrently deleted between the SELECT and the UPDATE, the scalar update silently no-ops and the script reports `processed++`. Not high-likelihood, but an `executeTakeFirst()`-style affected-rows check + warn would surface real anomalies.\n\n---\n\n## What's done well\n\n- `upsertFacets` correctly wraps DELETE+INSERT in a single transaction (`analyses.ts:1261`) so partial-state reads are impossible **within** the facet tables.\n- The `markComplete`-returns-false test (`analysis-queue.test.ts:655`) explicitly pins that superseded workers don't double-write — good guard against last-writer races.\n- Empty-input short-circuits in `aggregateFacetsByCheckpointSet` and `aggregateFacetsByAgent` (`analyses.ts:1347, 1373`) avoid generating SQL like `IN ()` which would otherwise blow up — and the tests pin both.\n- `mcpServersUsed` count filtering (`analysis-queue.ts:723-725`, backfill `backfill-analysis-facets.ts:245-247`) prevents zero-count rows from ever reaching the table.\n\nRelevant files:\n- `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/entire.io/.worktrees/recap-endpoint/api/src/lib/analysis-queue.ts`\n- `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/entire.io/.worktrees/recap-endpoint/api/src/routes/recap.ts`\n- `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/entire.io/.worktrees/recap-endpoint/api/src/lib/planetscale/analyses.ts`\n- `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/entire.io/.worktrees/recap-endpoint/api/scripts/backfill-analysis-facets.ts`\n- `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/entire.io/.worktrees/recap-endpoint/api/db/migrations/20260429100000_add_analysis_facet_tables.ts`"},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a3791144f9e6bcaa6 (use SendMessage with to: 'a3791144f9e6bcaa6' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 70611\ntool