{"parentUuid":"3d8284e2-7f5b-4c33-b7e4-378520ee7517","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01YFZFbHKSCGorsA1yy8FpTP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sharp question. Let me separate two different failure modes that both end with \"wrong numbers in recap.\"\n\n## Two distinct ways recap could show wrong numbers\n\n**Mode A: SQL aggregator code has a bug**\n- The new aggregation code returns wrong numbers\n- Performance is good (it's still fast), but the data is wrong\n- Recovery: roll back to the legacy in-JS aggregator\n- What we reverted that affected this: the **env-driven flag**. With the flag, rollback was an env flip (seconds). With the const, rollback is a deploy (minutes-to-an-hour).\n\n**Mode B: Worker crashes mid-write between two writes**\n- Worker writes facets, crashes before writing scalars\n- Database has fresh facets next to stale `transcript_tokens=0`\n- Recap then reads inconsistent rows\n- What we reverted that affected this: the **torn-write fix** (`persistAnalysisAggregates` atomic combined method)\n\n## Did we revert something we shouldn't have?\n\nHonest answer: **no, but we did walk back from \"structurally impossible\" to \"monitor and fix when observed\" for both modes.**\n\nThe performance fix itself — the actual SQL aggregation that makes `/me/recap` go from 60s to 0.7-1.9s — is intact and partial.to-validated. We didn't touch that. Performance works.\n\nWhat we reverted are the **safety nets around the new code**, not the new code itself:\n\n| Reverted | What it actually did | What it didn't do |\n|---|---|---|\n| `persistAnalysisAggregates` atomic write | Made torn-write structurally impossible | Did not fix or affect performance |\n| Env-driven `ENTIRE_RECAP_LEGACY_AGGREGATOR` | Made rollback an env flip instead of a deploy | Did not improve correctness — just recovery speed |\n| Parity test | Would have caught legacy-vs-SQL divergence on one fixture | Was hand-derived (editorial), so didn't actually verify aggregator correctness in general |\n\n## Your instinct is half right\n\nThe revert pass said \"we accept theoretical-risk failure modes in exchange for not paying engineering cost.\" That's true for both Mode A and Mode B.\n\nFor **Mode A** (SQL aggregator bug): nothing we reverted would have *prevented* a bug. The env flag we reverted only made *recovery* faster. So if the SQL aggregator has a bug, you find out the same way you would with or without the flag — the difference is just rollback time.\n\nFor **Mode B** (torn write): the reverted fix would have made this structurally impossible. By going back to two writes, we accept the failure mode at its theoretical probability.\n\n## Should anything come back?\n\nIf I had to put one revert back, it would be the **torn-write fix** (`persistAnalysisAggregates`). Reasoning:\n- It's a structural correctness improvement, not a speed improvement\n- The pattern (one transaction for atomically-related writes) is what the code should look like even without an incident\n- ~125 lines is real but the value is \"this class of bug can never happen\" rather than \"this class of bug becomes faster to detect\"\n- The env flag was about ops convenience for a rollback path nobody plans to use; the torn-write fix is about not having an inconsistent-state failure mode in the first place\n\nThe env flag and parity test, on the other hand, I'd leave reverted — neither improves the code's actual behavior, they only improve detection/recovery speed for failure modes that haven't materialized.\n\nWant me to put `persistAnalysisAggregates` back? It's roughly +100 lines and would close Mode B structurally rather than monitorably. Trade-off is +100 lines + an extra test on the perf PR."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2508,"cache_read_input_tokens":617283,"output_tokens":3449,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":