actToolMetrics` would have silently zeroed (because Codex never emits `type: \"assistant\"` JSONL frames). The inline comment about the all-zeros bug makes the test's intent unmistakable for future readers. Good defensive test design.\n- **Unknown-agent test exercises the fallback path explicitly.** Hand-rolling a `TranscriptMessage` with `toolCalls: [{ id: \"1\", name: \"anything\", input: {}, timestamp: \"\" }]` validates that `FallbackParser` (no optional methods) routes through `?? \"other\"` correctly. Without this test, regressions to that fallback would only surface in production.\n- **Old code fully removed.** No commented-out `extractToolMetrics`, no orphaned `buildTranscript()` helper, no leftover `StoredTranscriptSession` import. The diff is \"delete the old, add the new\" with nothing in between.\n\n## Issues\n\n### Critical\n\nNone.\n\n### Important\n\n**1. Import order regression in `analysis-queue.ts`.** The new `import { getParserForAgent } from \"./transcript-parsers/registry\"` is placed at **line 35**, breaking the alphabetical order of the surrounding relative imports:\n\n```\n33: import { invalidateCheckpointCaches } from \"./kv\"\n34: import { getOrCreatePostHogClient, isFeatureEnabled } from \"./posthog\"\n35: import { getParserForAgent } from \"./transcript-parsers/registry\"   ← misplaced\n36: import { avgMs, errorString, generateBatchId } from \"./telemetry\"\n37: import { getInstallationToken } from \"./token\"\n```\n\nThe rest of the file is alphabetized (`db` → `db/checkpoints` → `db/db-types` → `entire-settings` → `kv` → `posthog` → `telemetry` → `token`). `transcript-parsers/registry` should sort *after* `token`. oxlint doesn't catch this (no import-order rule in `oxlint.base.json`), but the previous import (`stored-transcript`) was correctly slotted before `telemetry`, so this is a fresh ordering regression introduced by this commit. Trivial to fix; worth fixing because the rest of the file is consistent.\n\n**2. Imports placed mid-file in `analysis-queue.test.ts`.** Lines 126-128 add three `import` statements *after* the first `describe(...)` block:\n\n```\n124: })\n125:\n126: import { ClaudeCodeParser } from \"./transcript-parsers/claude-code-parser\"\n127: import { CodexParser } from \"./transcript-parsers/codex-parser\"\n128: import { extractToolMetricsFromSessions } from \"./analysis-queue\"\n```\n\nESM hoists these so the runtime is fine, and oxlint isn't configured with `import/first`, so this passes lint. But it's stylistic clutter — the file already has a top-of-file import block at lines 1-11 and `extractToolMetricsFromSessions` could have been added to that existing `from \"./analysis-queue\"` block. Move all three imports to the top alongside the existing imports. Pure mechanical cleanup, no behavior change.\n\n### Minor\n\n**3. `ParsedSessionForMetrics` could derive from `SessionTranscript`.** The new interface picks two fields out of `SessionTranscript`:\n\n```ts\nexport interface ParsedSessionForMetrics {\n  agent: string\n  messages: TranscriptMessage[]\n}\n```\n\nSince the only caller (`decorateReviewWithMetrics`) passes `loaded.sessions: SessionTranscript[]` directly, an equivalent definition is `Pick<SessionTranscript, \"agent\" | \"messages\">`. That keeps the metric-extractor structurally compatible with `SessionTranscript` automatically if `agent` ever becomes a non-`string` (e.g., a tagged union or branded type). This is a minor type-hygiene win; the explicit interface is also fine. Mention only because the file already uses `Pick<LoadedCheckpointTranscript, ...>` for `decorateReviewWithMetrics` two functions up — using the same pattern here would be consistent.\n\n**4. `extractToolMetricsFromSessions` is exported but the export isn't used outside this file + its test.** It's declared `export function` at module scope. The only production caller is the same file's `decorateReviewWithMetrics`. The export exists so the test can import it. That's fine — it matches the test's 