ports to the top alongside the existing imports. Pure mechanical cleanup, no behavior change.\n\n### Minor\n\n**3. `ParsedSessionForMetrics` could derive from `SessionTranscript`.** The new interface picks two fields out of `SessionTranscript`:\n\n```ts\nexport interface ParsedSessionForMetrics {\n  agent: string\n  messages: TranscriptMessage[]\n}\n```\n\nSince the only caller (`decorateReviewWithMetrics`) passes `loaded.sessions: SessionTranscript[]` directly, an equivalent definition is `Pick<SessionTranscript, \"agent\" | \"messages\">`. That keeps the metric-extractor structurally compatible with `SessionTranscript` automatically if `agent` ever becomes a non-`string` (e.g., a tagged union or branded type). This is a minor type-hygiene win; the explicit interface is also fine. Mention only because the file already uses `Pick<LoadedCheckpointTranscript, ...>` for `decorateReviewWithMetrics` two functions up — using the same pattern here would be consistent.\n\n**4. `extractToolMetricsFromSessions` is exported but the export isn't used outside this file + its test.** It's declared `export function` at module scope. The only production caller is the same file's `decorateReviewWithMetrics`. The export exists so the test can import it. That's fine — it matches the test's import — but it's worth knowing the function is effectively private to this module. Not actionable; flagging only because if you ever want to make `decorateReviewWithMetrics` itself testable you'd want to export *that* and could un-export this one. Plan-aligned for now.\n\n**5. Empty-string `agent` case isn't covered.** `getParserForAgent(\"\")` normalizes to `\"\"`, misses every key in `parsers`, and returns `fallbackParser`. So an empty agent string would route through the unknown-agent fallback (`?? \"other\"` for everything). This is fine behaviorally, but worth noting that the existing test only covers `\"UnknownAgent\"`. If `LoadedCheckpointTranscript.sessions[].agent` is ever empty (e.g., a sentinel for \"agent not yet detected\"), it'll still classify cleanly. Don't add a test — just confirming it's robust.\n\n## Things Verified, No Issue Found\n\n- **Type safety of the function signature.** `ParsedSessionForMetrics` requires `agent: string` and `messages: TranscriptMessage[]`. Passing `SessionTranscript[]` works structurally because `SessionTranscript` is a superset. The width is appropriate — narrowing further (e.g., requiring `tokenUsage`) would reject valid inputs.\n- **`?? \"other\"` default is the right call.** Per the plan, agents without `classifyTool` are deliberately silent-defaulted to avoid logspam during the rollout. A real-world failure mode would require a parser that *should* implement `classifyTool` but forgot — but that's caught by per-parser unit tests (e.g., `claude-code-parser.test.ts` line 318+, `codex-parser.test.ts` line 464+, `gemini-parser.test.ts` line 10+). No production telemetry needed at this surface.\n- **Test parser coupling.** Using `new ClaudeCodeParser().parse(jsonl)` to produce `TranscriptMessage[]` is acceptable. The alternative — hand-building `TranscriptMessage` objects — is what the unknown-agent test does at line 215, and it works for that case but would be unmaintainable for the Claude/Codex tests where the parser's `tool_use` extraction is what's being indirectly validated. The plan's choice is correct: real parsers > fixture maintenance.\n- **Silent-failure surfaces.** `messages.toolCalls ?? []` and the optional-method fallbacks gracefully skip missing data. None of these mask real bugs because the per-parser tests validate that `toolCalls` is populated correctly upstream.\n- **`decorateReviewWithMetrics` signature change.** Confirmed single private caller (`generateAndStoreReview` at line 326). The narrowed `Pick<LoadedCheckpointTranscript, \"sessions\" | \"sessionMetadata\">` is structurally compatible with the full `LoadedCheckpointTranscript` the caller passes. No API break.\n- **Tests pass.** 28/28 in `