{"parentUuid":"2fabc6fd-59e5-4f62-9eaa-92117fda5903","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"5b75148f-df73-4605-b328-6daafc75d92d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QESb6o7yWwH6FRMyHcFKGm","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Good — `findCheckpointById` defaults `defaultBranch = \"main\"`. Passing `undefined` is fine. Now a final detail check: the cursor advances even on \"empty after filter\" batches because `batch.length === 0` `break`s out of the loop. That is correct exit behavior — but worth noting this code does not have a \"small last batch\" issue since it queries with `limit(BATCH_SIZE)` and exits at batch.length === 0.\n\nLet me verify one more thing: the merge semantics test asserts `written.toolProfile.categories.shell.count` and `fileOps.count`. The Codex agent dispatcher in Task 5 should classify `exec_command` as shell and `apply_patch` as fileOps. That's a real assertion that would catch a regression in the Task 5 wiring.\n\nNow let me check that the cursor scenario where every row in a batch is filtered (e.g., all SyntaxError) still advances correctly. Looking at the loop: `processed++` happens unconditionally inside the for-loop, and `cursor` is set from `batch[batch.length - 1]`, which is independent of skip/process status. So even an all-skipped batch advances the cursor — correct.\n\nOne subtle issue: if the *last* batch ever contains 0 rows after filtering and the initial query returns < BATCH_SIZE, we still loop one more time (because `processed` advanced but the next query returns []). That's fine — we break on `batch.length === 0`.\n\nBut what about: `processed >= max` check happens at the top of the while loop, but `processed++` happens per row inside the for-loop. So if `max=5` and the first batch has 500 rows, we'll process all 500 before the next iteration check. Let me re-confirm.\n\nYes — the `--limit=N` flag truncates only at the *while* boundary, not mid-batch. With BATCH_SIZE=500, `-