{"parentUuid":"3284c1a9-b3d3-4aea-a711-2bd71a32c150","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"99d54480-a139-4656-8e51-14cd73c0caa3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NPq9MtA8JtxvgX1sPRcY9Z","type":"tool_result","content":"# Setup\n\n## 1. Install Dependencies\n\n```bash\npnpm install\n```\n\n## 2. Configure Secrets\n\nInstall 1Password CLI and populate dev secrets from the shared vault:\n\n```bash\nbrew install 1password-cli\n```\n\n```bash\nmise run secrets\n```\n\nSecrets are stored in 1Password (Shared vault, item `entirehq/entire.io`).\n\n<details>\n<summary>Creating a new GitHub App (if needed)</summary>\n\n1. Create at https://github.com/settings/apps/new\n1. Callback URL: `http://localhost:8787/auth/github/callback`. Enable \"Expire user authorization tokens\", uncheck \"Request user authorization (OAuth) during installation\" and \"Enable Device Flow\"\n1. Webhook URL needs to be accessible over public internet so use NGrok or something and should be `$host/api/v1/webhooks/github`\n1. Repository permissions: Contents > Read-only\n1. No event subscriptions; only allow on \"this account\" (your Github account)\n\n</details>\n\n## 3. Set Up MySQL (Local - Recommended)\n\nWe use a local MySQL Docker container for dev and tests.\n\n**Prerequisites:** Docker must be installed and running. Choose one:\n\n**Option A: Colima (lightweight, recommended for macOS)**\n\nColima is a free, open-source tool that runs Docker containers inside a lightweight Linux VM. It's recommended over Docker Desktop because it uses fewer resources and avoids Docker Desktop's commercial license requirements for larger organizations, but it's CLI-only with no GUI dashboard.\n\n```bash\nbrew install colima docker docker-compose\ncolima start\n```\n\n**Option B: [Docker Desktop](https://www.docker.com/products/docker-desktop/)**\n\n**Option C: [OrbStack](https://orbstack.dev/)**\n\n---\n\n```bash\n# Start local DB\nmise run api-db-start\n\n# Create DB and apply migrations\nmise run api-db-reset\n\n# To just run migrations (i.e., don't blow everything away)\nmise run api-db-migrate\n```\n\n## 4. Run the Dev Server\n\n```bash\nmise run dev\n```\n\nThe server will be accessible at `http://localhost:5173`.\n\n## 5. PostHog (Analytics & Toolbar)\n\nPostHog env vars are populated by `mise run secrets` into `frontend/.env` and `website/.env`.\n\nTo use the PostHog toolbar locally (inspect elements, heatmaps, feature flags), launch it from:\nhttps://eu.posthog.com/project/102418/toolbar\n\nThe dev CSP in `vite.config.ts` allows both `42.entire.io` and `*.posthog.com` for the toolbar. Production routes everything through the `42.entire.io` proxy.\n\n## 6. Configure Hyperdrive (Staging/Production)\n\nCloudflare Hyperdrive accelerates MySQL connections from Workers. First, in Planetscale, create a\npassword. It must be \"admin\" because it should be allowed to run DDL operations. Then, create a\nHyperdrive config for each environment:\n\n```bash\n# Staging (default wrangler env)\npnpm exec wrangler hyperdrive create entire-db-staging \\\n  --connection-string=\"REDACTED\"\n\n# Production\npnpm exec wrangler hyperdrive create entire-db-production \\\n  --connection-string=\"REDACTED\"\n```\n\nThen update the Hyperdrive IDs in `api/wrangler.jsonc`:\n\n- Default environment: replace `STAGING_HYPERDRIVE_ID` with the staging ID\n- `env.production`: replace `PRODUCTION_HYPERDRIVE_ID` with the production ID\n\n**Running migrations against staging/production:**\n\nThe migration runner uses `DATABASE_URL` to connect directly to PlanetScale (not via Hyperdrive, which is for Workers only). Get the connection string from the PlanetScale dashboard under your branch's connection settings.\n\n## 7. Restore from a PlanetScale Backup\n\nIf you need to switch to a backup (e.g., after a bad migration):\n\n1. Go to [PlanetScale backups](https://app.planetscale.com/entire/partial-to/backups) and pick one\n2. Click \"Restore to new branch\" and pick a size (smallest is enough)\n3. Stay on the page until the branch is ready\n4. Go to [branches](https://app.planetscale.com/entire/partial-to/branches) and make the new branch the default\n5. Click the new branch, then \"Connect\" (top right)\n6. Generate new passwords:\n   - For migrations: role must be **admin** (needs DDL)\n   - For the Worker: **Read/Write** is enough\n7. Update credentials in two places:\n\n**Hyperdrive** (Worker connection): go to [Hyperdrive settings](https://dash.cloudflare.com/8b04592ed74a5cce30d355b07276caf3/workers/hyperdrive/e55646c03c454ba2a20ae7357c4b9f4b/settings). Hit the \"Edit\" button above the table (don't try to edit individual values).\n\n**GitHub Actions** (migration runner): go to [action secrets](https://github.com/entirehq/entire.io/settings/secrets/actions) and update `PLANETSCALE_DATABASE_URL_PARTIAL`. The format is:\n\n```\nREDACTED\n```\n\n## 8. Deploy frontend changes to Staging\n\nStaging access must be requested from a Cloudflare admin.\n\nYou can see a full list of deployments and logs in the [Cloudflare dashboard](https://dash.cloudflare.com/8b04592ed74a5cce30d355b07276caf3/pages/view/partial-to-frontend).\n\nTo kick off a staging deploy locally, run the following from the `frontend/` directory:\n\n```bash\nVITE_API_BASE_URL=https://partial.to pnpm run build && pnpm exec wrangler pages deploy dist --project-name=partial-to-frontend --branch=main\n```\n\n> **Why `--branch=main`?** Cloudflare Pages uses the branch name to determine which deployment is the \"production\" deployment for the project. The `partial-to-frontend` Pages project treats `main` as its production branch. If you pass your actual working branch name instead, Cloudflare will create a preview deployment at a different URL rather than updating the primary staging deployment. This is a Cloudflare Pages convention — `--branch=main` here refers to the Pages project's production branch, not your git branch.\n\n## 7. Chrome DevTools MCP (Optional)\n\nLets Claude Code or Codex interact with a running Chrome instance — inspect pages, run performance traces, execute scripts, take screenshots.\n\n**Prerequisites:** Chrome >= M144\n\n1. In Chrome, navigate to `chrome://inspect/#remote-debugging` and allow incoming debugging connections\n2. For Claude Code, `.mcp.json` already includes the `chrome-devtools` server\n3. For Codex, `.codex/config.toml` already includes the `chrome-devtools` server for this project. If you prefer a per-user setup instead, run `codex mcp add chrome-devtools -- pnpm dlx chrome-devtools-mcp@latest --autoConnect --slim`\n4. Trust the project in Codex if prompted, restart your client if it is already running, then test by asking it to check the performance of a URL\n\n> **Note:** Chrome must be running before starting Claude Code or Codex. Connects to the default profile. A permission dialog is shown on first connection.\n\n### Slim vs Full mode\n\nThe default config uses `--slim`, which exposes 3 core tools:\n\n- **navigate** — load a URL\n- **evaluate** — run JavaScript in page context\n- **screenshot** — capture the page\n\nThis covers most dev tasks since `evaluate` can do almost anything programmatically, and keeps token usage low.\n\nRemove `--slim` from `.mcp.json` and `.codex/config.toml` to enable all 29 tools when you need:\n\n- **Input automation** (9) — click, drag, fill, fill_form, hover, press_key, type_text, upload_file, handle_dialog\n- **Navigation** (6) — multi-tab management, wait_for, history traversal\n- **Emulation** (2) — device emulation (dark mode, throttling, geolocation, network conditions), viewport resize\n- **Performance** (4) — start/stop traces, Core Web Vitals analysis, heap snapshots\n- **Network** (2) — list and inspect network requests with bodies\n- **Debugging** (6) — console messages, Lighthouse audits, accessibility tree snapshots","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"c31a38c8-f33e-44f8-af70-989295611436","timestamp":"2026-05-01T19:35:04.505Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# Setup\n\n## 1. Install Dependencies\n\n```bash\npnpm install\n```\n\n## 2. Configure Secrets\n\nInstall 1Password CLI and populate dev secrets from the shared vault:\n\n```bash\nbrew install 1password-cli\n```\n\n```bash\nmise run secrets\n```\n\nSecrets are stored in 1Password (Shared vault, item `entirehq/entire.io`).\n\n<details>\n<summary>Creating a new GitHub App (if needed)</summary>\n\n1. Create at https://github.com/settings/apps/new\n1. Callback URL: `http://localhost:8787/auth/github/callback`. Enable \"Expire user authorization tokens\", uncheck \"Request user authorization (OAuth) during installation\" and \"Enable Device Flow\"\n1. Webhook URL needs to be accessible over public internet so use NGrok or something and should be `$host/api/v1/webhooks/github`\n1. Repository permissions: Contents > Read-only\n1. No event subscriptions; only allow on \"this account\" (your Github account)\n\n</details>\n\n## 3. Set Up MySQL (Local - Recommended)\n\nWe use a local MySQL Docker container for dev and tests.\n\n**Prerequisites:** Docker must be installed and running. Choose one:\n\n**Option A: Colima (lightweight, recommended for macOS)**\n\nColima is a free, open-source tool that runs Docker containers inside a lightweight Linux VM. It's recommended over Docker Desktop because it uses fewer resources and avoids Docker Desktop's commercial license requirements for larger organizations, but it's CLI-only with no GUI dashboard.\n\n```bash\nbrew install colima docker docker-compose\ncolima start\n```\n\n**Option B: [Docker Desktop](https://www.docker.com/products/docker-desktop/)**\n\n**Option C: [OrbStack](https://orbstack.dev/)**\n\n---\n\n```bash\n# Start local DB\nmise run api-db-start\n\n# Create DB and apply migrations\nmise run api-db-reset\n\n# To just run migrations (i.e., don't blow everything away)\nmise run api-db-migrate\n```\n\n## 4. Run the Dev Server\n\n```bash\nmise run dev\n```\n\nThe server will be accessible at `http://localhost:5173`.\n\n## 5.