 passwords:\n   - For migrations: role must be **admin** (needs DDL)\n   - For the Worker: **Read/Write** is enough\n7. Update credentials in two places:\n\n**Hyperdrive** (Worker connection): go to [Hyperdrive settings](https://dash.cloudflare.com/8b04592ed74a5cce30d355b07276caf3/workers/hyperdrive/e55646c03c454ba2a20ae7357c4b9f4b/settings). Hit the \"Edit\" button above the table (don't try to edit individual values).\n\n**GitHub Actions** (migration runner): go to [action secrets](https://github.com/entirehq/entire.io/settings/secrets/actions) and update `PLANETSCALE_DATABASE_URL_PARTIAL`. The format is:\n\n```\nREDACTED\n```\n\n## 8. Deploy frontend changes to Staging\n\nStaging access must be requested from a Cloudflare admin.\n\nYou can see a full list of deployments and logs in the [Cloudflare dashboard](https://dash.cloudflare.com/8b04592ed74a5cce30d355b07276caf3/pages/view/partial-to-frontend).\n\nTo kick off a staging deploy locally, run the following from the `frontend/` directory:\n\n```bash\nVITE_API_BASE_URL=https://partial.to pnpm run build && pnpm exec wrangler pages deploy dist --project-name=partial-to-frontend --branch=main\n```\n\n> **Why `--branch=main`?** Cloudflare Pages uses the branch name to determine which deployment is the \"production\" deployment for the project. The `partial-to-frontend` Pages project treats `main` as its production branch. If you pass your actual working branch name instead, Cloudflare will create a preview deployment at a different URL rather than updating the primary staging deployment. This is a Cloudflare Pages convention — `--branch=main` here refers to the Pages project's production branch, not your git branch.\n\n## 7. Chrome DevTools MCP (Optional)\n\nLets Claude Code or Codex interact with a running Chrome instance — inspect pages, run performance traces, execute scripts, take screenshots.\n\n**Prerequisites:** Chrome >= M144\n\n1. In Chrome, navigate to `chrome://inspect/#remote-debugging` and allow incoming debugging connections\n2. For Claude Code, `.mcp.json` already includes the `chrome-devtools` server\n3. For Codex, `.codex/config.toml` already includes the `chrome-devtools` server for this project. If you prefer a per-user setup instead, run `codex mcp add chrome-devtools -- pnpm dlx chrome-devtools-mcp@latest --autoConnect --slim`\n4. Trust the project in Codex if prompted, restart your client if it is already running, then test by asking it to check the performance of a URL\n\n> **Note:** Chrome must be running before starting Claude Code or Codex. Connects to the default profile. A permission dialog is shown on first connection.\n\n### Slim vs Full mode\n\nThe default config uses `--slim`, which exposes 3 core tools:\n\n- **navigate** — load a URL\n- **evaluate** — run JavaScript in page context\n- **screenshot** — capture the page\n\nThis covers most dev tasks since `evaluate` can do almost anything programmatically, and keeps token usage low.\n\nRemove `--slim` from `.mcp.json` and `.codex/config.toml` to enable all 29 tools when you need:\n\n- **Input automation** (9) — click, drag, fill, fill_form, hover, press_key, type_text, upload_file, handle_dialog\n- **Navigation** (6) — multi-tab management, wait_for, history traversal\n- **Emulation** (2) — device emulation (dark mode, throttling, geolocation, network conditions), viewport resize\n- **Performance** (4) — start/stop traces, Core Web Vitals analysis, heap snapshots\n- **Network** (2) — list and inspect network requests with bodies\n- **Debugging** (6) — console messages, Lighthouse audits, accessibility tree snapshots","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3284c1a9-b3d3-4aea-a711-2bd71a32c150","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/entire-activity","sessionId":"a654b6ca-4957-4d7c-8c99-62db313ff0a0","version":"2.1.119","gitBranch":"entire-activity","slug":"vector